From: aeb@cwi.nl (Andries Brouwer)
Subject: `Sticky' modifier keys in keyboard driver
Date: 1995/10/24
Message-ID: <cola-liw-814559777-28590-1@oravannahka.helsinki.fi>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118037322
approved: linux-announce@news.ornl.gov (Lars Wirzenius)
followup-to: comp.os.linux.development.system
content-type: text/plain; charset=ISO-8859-1
organization: CWI, Amsterdam
keywords: kbd
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.os.linux.announce

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Until recently, the kernel had only little support for people
who can use only one finger for typing (or use a mouthstick).

The most common solution in such cases was to use ShiftLock,
CtrlLock etc, so that typing ^C required the three keystrokes
CtrlLock C CtrlLock (assuming that the next symbol is not a
control character).  [And even this possibility, although
documented in the Keyboard FAQ, was unknown to almost everybody.]

Since 1.3.33 the kernel also has the concept of `sticky'
modifier keys (like the dead keys used for diacritics).
Such a key acts on the first non-sticky key:
the three keystrokes SCtrl SAlt F2 act like Ctrl-Alt-F2.

The details will be documented in kbd-0.91, but since that
will not be released this week or next, and several people
have expressed great interest, this preliminary announcement.

As an example,
        % loadkeys << EOF
        keymaps 0-15
        keycode 54 = 0x0c00
        keycode 97 = 0x0c02
        keycode 100 = 0x0c03
        EOF
will make the right Shift, Ctrl, Alt sticky versions of the left ones.

Note that 1.3.33 does not compile and 1.3.34 does not work, so if
you are eager to try this out, pick up 1.3.35, which seems somewhat
stable.


